# ── Local-only working artifacts — repo is CODE-ONLY (no images/exports ever) ──
# Root-scoped (/*) on purpose: ignores the loose screenshots/exports that pile up at the
# repo root WITHOUT reaching into subdirs (e.g. audit/) that other tooling may own. The only
# intentional image is the base64-inline logo, so "no committed root images" is the invariant.
/*.png
/*.docx
Strategies (Trading View)/

# Audit/review artifacts (baseline, ledger, surfacing + source-data specs, logs) — local-only
audit/

# Local-only tool test: imports the gitignored tools/snapshot.py, so committing it would
# break every public clone. Ignored IN PLACE — pytest still collects it locally (count stays
# 1693); it just can never leak into the code-only public repo. (paired with tools/ above)
tests/test_snapshot_tool.py

# WAVE 3.0 lives in alpha/ as its OWN standalone git repo (github.com/KHD125/WAVE-3.0).
# PRISM must never track it — fully separate systems by owner decision (2026-08-23).
alpha/
Wave (2.0)/

# RS-Stages is a SEPARATE project (github.com/Pareshking/RS-Stages) unzipped
# inside this tree. It has no .git of its own, so PRISM sees it as untracked
# files -- one "git add -A" would commit an entire other project into PRISM,
# which auto-deploys. Same reason alpha/ and Wave (2.0)/ are ignored.
RS-Stages/

# Pasted UI screenshots used for audits, and throwaway render harnesses. Both are working
# artifacts, not deliverables: the repo is deliberately code-only (Other Resources/, docs/ and
# every visual-check *.png above are ignored for the same reason). Screenshot/ escaped /*.png
# only by being a subdirectory, and its contents are renders of the gitignored CSV data.
Screenshot/
_scratch_*.py
stockscans_sync/
# Kite Algo is a separate private project with its own git repo (khd343/Kite-Algo) and its own
# .env secrets. It lives inside this folder but must never enter this PUBLIC repo.
Kite Algo/
# Lens is a separate private project with its own git repo (khd343/Lens): Kite's read-only window.
Lens/
# Paresh-main is a downloaded copy of Paresh's app (MIT, © Pareshking): Lens's design reference, kept here
